        <head><ref type="lawCode" target="law:InCn">In Cn</ref> III 56</head>
        <ab type="liebermannText" xml:lang="la" source="#liebermann" n="In Cn III 56" corresp="#wit-lat"><pb n="615" ed="#liebermann"/>In lege Cantia<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="4" target="#InCn_III-56.lnote.oe.4"/> Ęcclesia Christi et rex et archiepiscopus habent similem et ęque carum despectum, quod Angli dicunt mundbrecc<ptr type="liebermannNote" n="5" target="#InCn_III-56.lnote.oe.5"/>.</ab>
        <note type="comparison" source="#wormald-mel" n="394"><label>derives from</label> <ref target="clause:Grith_Grid-Inscr">Grið</ref> <quote>although it was perhaps associated with it in the source of Instituta Cnuti. [n.] Inst. Cn III 56 – 56:2</quote></note>
        <note type="editorial">New row (October 2026): Liebermann’s text transcribed from Gesetze I p. 615 (two independent readings of the page images, differences settled against the page)</note>
